.TH LINUXCNCTOP "1"  "2026-07-31" "LinuxCNC Documentation" "The Enhanced Machine Controller"
.SH NAME
linuxcnctop \- show the LinuxCNC machine status
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B linuxcnctop
[\fI\-\-url URL\fR] [\fI\-\-title TITLE\fR] [\fI\-\-width W\fR] [\fI\-\-height H\fR] [\fI\-\-instance NAME\fR]
.br
.B linuxcnctop \-t
[\fI\-\-json\fR] [\fI\-n SEC\fR] [\fI\-\-full\fR] [\fI\-\-instance NAME\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBlinuxcnctop\fR shows the LinuxCNC state in a format similar to the Linux
"top" command: one row per status field, with recently changed values
highlighted.

With no arguments it opens the status window, replacing itself with
\fBlinuxcnctop\-ui\fR(1). With \fB\-t\fR it prints the status to standard output
instead \(em for machines without a display, for scripts, and for pasting state
into a bug report. Any text\-mode option (\fB\-\-json\fR, \fB\-n\fR,
\fB\-\-full\fR) implies \fB\-t\fR.

The text output is one \fIname value\fR line per status field, using the classic
field names \fBlinuxcnc.stat()\fR always exposed. \fB\-\-json\fR prints the raw
status snapshot instead, whose field paths are the ones the window displays.

Lengths are reported in millimetres, the unit the controller keeps internally,
regardless of the machine's configured units.

If the controller cannot be reached, \fBlinuxcnctop\fR reports the failure on
standard error and exits non\-zero rather than printing a stale or empty
machine.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-text\fR
Print the status as text instead of opening the window.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print the raw \fIStatFull\fR snapshot from the REST API as indented JSON.
Implies \fB\-t\fR.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-interval\fR \fISEC\fR
Repeat every \fISEC\fR seconds until interrupted. Implies \fB\-t\fR. In this
mode an unreachable controller is reported and waited out rather than being
fatal, so the command can be started before the machine is up.
.TP
\fB\-\-full\fR
Do not truncate values to 58 columns. Implies \fB\-t\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-url\fR \fIURL\fR, \fB\-\-title\fR \fITITLE\fR, \fB\-\-width\fR \fIW\fR, \fB\-\-height\fR \fIH\fR
Passed through to \fBlinuxcnctop\-ui\fR(1) when opening the window.
.TP
\fB\-\-instance\fR \fINAME\fR
Query the named task instance. Defaults to \fB$STMAK_TASK_INSTANCE\fR, or
\fImilltask\fR if that is unset.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B STMAK_REST_URL
Base URL of the controller's REST API (default http://127.0.0.1:5080).
.TP
.B STMAK_TASK_INSTANCE
Default task instance name.
.SH EXAMPLES
Open the status window (what AXIS's Machine > Show LinuxCNC Status does, though
it calls \fBlinuxcnctop\-ui\fR directly):
.PP
.RS
linuxcnctop
.RE
.PP
Print the status once:
.PP
.RS
linuxcnctop \-t
.RE
.PP
Watch the status once a second, untruncated:
.PP
.RS
linuxcnctop \-n 1 \-\-full
.RE
.PP
Capture the raw snapshot for a bug report:
.PP
.RS
linuxcnctop \-\-json > status.json
.RE
.SH NOTES
It is more fully documented in the AXIS GUI documentation but can be run
standalone or with other GUIs.
http://linuxcnc.org/docs/html/gui/axis.html
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBlinuxcnctop\-ui(1)\fR, \fBstmakui(1)\fR, \fBLinuxCNC(1)\fR

Much more information about LinuxCNC and HAL is available in the LinuxCNC
and HAL User Manuals, found at /usr/share/doc/LinuxCNC/.

